    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay got it. so if the lisinopril is basically just stopping that conversion step, how exactly does the drug molecule physically interact with the ACE enzyme to stop it from working? is it a competitive inhibition thing or something else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril and similar drugs are called competitive inhibitors because they bind to the active site of the ACE enzyme, where Ang II would typically bind. The binding of lisinopril to the ACE’s active site is similar in structure to that of Ang II, which allows lisinopril to effectively compete for the same binding site.\n\nOnce LISINOPRIL is bound to the active site of ACE, it blocks the access of Ang II to the same site and prevents its conversion to angiotensin II. As a result, the ACE can no longer perform its normal function of converting Ang I to Ang II, meaning that high levels of angiotensin II aren't produced, thus leading to lower blood pressure by reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion. This mechanism of competitive inhibition is effective in managing hypertension through lowering blood pressure levels.",
      "source": "target",
